WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:15.480
We're going to describe a class of tests called permutation tests, and in particular two-sample

00:15.480 --> 00:25.620
permutation tests. Now permutation tests are a family of tests which are at least when sample

00:25.620 --> 00:38.280
sizes are not small, they're computationally intensive. Luckily today we have software

00:38.280 --> 00:51.660
available and the software package R provides commands for performing permutation tests.

00:51.660 --> 01:04.900
Well, we'll illustrate with an example. An example is a US trial, a civil trial, a discrimination

01:04.900 --> 01:18.900
case from 1981, and the plaintiff was named Andrea Capacci, and she brought a case to the

01:18.900 --> 01:31.900
court against her employers, Katz and Besthoff, Inc. Now you can Google this trial if you

01:31.900 --> 01:40.900
like. And this trial was a sexual discrimination trial, and as part of the evidence there were

01:40.900 --> 02:01.900
data presented about times to promotion in months for men and women in the company over a period

02:01.900 --> 02:13.900
of time. So, for the men's times to promotion, the figures in order were something like this,

02:13.900 --> 02:27.900
5, 7, 12, 40, 40, won't give the full data here. There were 22 promotion times for men, and the

02:27.900 --> 02:39.900
sample size was 483. So let's say N1 sample size for the men was 22. There were only actually two women

02:39.900 --> 02:54.900
who got promoted, and their promotion times were 229 and 453. So, what would our null hypothesis be here? Well, we'll

02:54.900 --> 03:03.900
return to the notion of the extent of inference in general, just at the end of this little lecture. We

03:03.900 --> 03:10.900
noticed that really the data here are not samples, they represent the complete data that were available

03:10.900 --> 03:18.900
for individuals who were promoted over a particular time period. The time period itself is a form of a sample, but

03:18.900 --> 03:27.900
given the time period was determined by the duration of time that the plaintiff Kapachi had been in the

03:27.900 --> 03:36.900
employment of the company, that was kind of fixed. So, the process by which the data give rise to the

03:36.900 --> 03:44.900
the the process by which these promotion times are created, I suppose, is the the essence of what we're

03:44.900 --> 03:55.900
interested in. And whatever the processes were, they give rise to some data, we've observed the data. The

03:55.900 --> 04:20.900
null hypothesis would be that the distribution of times to promotion for men and women were the same over the

04:20.900 --> 04:30.900
time period in question. This would, of course, imply that they have the same means, the same medians.

04:30.900 --> 04:43.900
What would we take as the alternative hypothesis? Well, for sure, the distributions are different, which would

04:43.900 --> 04:48.900
simply be the negation of the null hypothesis. But I would argue here that one should consider only a

04:48.900 --> 05:08.900
one-sided alternative. The one-sided alternative being that the women's promotion times are greater, on average. So, the

05:08.900 --> 05:15.900
distribution of the distribution of the women's promotion times was, on average, larger. So, I had a greater

05:15.900 --> 05:29.900
mean, say, or greater median than that for the men. Now, why would I take a one-sided case here? I would argue that, given

05:29.900 --> 05:39.900
that this is a discrimination trial, it is inconceivable that a case would make it to the court for a claim for

05:39.900 --> 05:46.900
discrimination against women, gender discrimination, where in fact there was evidence that there was positive

05:46.900 --> 05:56.900
discrimination, or it was plausible, in any way, that there was positive discrimination towards women. Hence, I consider here the

05:56.900 --> 06:04.900
only logical alternative one can take to the null is an alternative which is one-sided. And that is the case that

06:04.900 --> 06:13.900
we will consider. So, what do we do in a permutation test? Well, we choose some test statistic or other. So, what should I

06:13.900 --> 06:23.900
choose as a test statistic? You can pretty much choose anything that would be a sensible choice. I'm going to choose something very

06:23.900 --> 06:44.900
simple. The sum of the women's promotion times, which in this case is 229 plus 453 is 682. That might strike you as

06:44.900 --> 06:50.900
being an odd choice for test statistic, just the sum of the data for the women. In fact, I'd get exactly the same inference

06:50.900 --> 06:58.900
inference here, if I chose the sample means, the difference in the sample means of the test statistic, or even the t

06:58.900 --> 07:05.900
statistic. So, if you were doing a two-sample t-test here, well, you've only got two observations for the women's group, but you

07:05.900 --> 07:19.900
could do a two-sample t-test, and I'd get the same inference, actually. I think I'm at it saying. So, the question we ask ourselves is, we have data,

07:19.900 --> 07:47.900
we have data for men, and we have data for women, and we have 22 observations for the men, and two for the women's, so 24 observations in total, two of which were women's, and 22 were men. So, we ask ourselves, well, how many, I'll say regroupings, are there, of 24 observations, where we put two in one group,

07:47.900 --> 08:09.900
and 22 in another? Well, the answer is, of course, 24 choose 2. So, 24 observations. I don't really care about the ordering in each group, I just care about how many ways I can put two in one group and 22 in another.

08:09.900 --> 08:24.500
And if we work that out, that comes to 276. So, there are 276 ways of taking 24 numbers and putting two in one group, call that the women, and 22 in the other.

08:24.500 --> 08:28.420
And under the null hypothesis, well, all of those regroupings should be equally likely.

08:30.920 --> 08:44.140
Now, the question that we ask is, how many of those give a value of the test statistic at least as inconsistent as the one that we've observed, which is 682?

08:44.140 --> 08:47.420
Well, we can enumerate them.

08:49.200 --> 08:54.620
In particular, the one that we observed, so we always include that one.

08:55.860 --> 08:59.480
So, that was the one that we observed. What else might we have observed?

08:59.480 --> 09:04.700
Well, we might have observed 229 and 483.

09:07.040 --> 09:15.380
We might have observed 453 and 483, the two largest values.

09:15.380 --> 09:34.980
So, those three ways to group our data to give a test statistic

09:34.980 --> 09:54.220
at least as inconsistent with the null hypothesis as the one that we observed.

10:01.220 --> 10:02.960
So, how do we find our p-value?

10:02.960 --> 10:09.240
Well, our p-value says, well, actually there were 276 ways,

10:09.640 --> 10:13.620
all of which should be equally likely of grouping the data,

10:14.240 --> 10:20.400
of which three gave a value at least as inconsistent as the one we observed.

10:20.520 --> 10:22.120
Of course, that includes the one we observed.

10:23.460 --> 10:28.860
And given that we're doing a one-tailed test,

10:28.860 --> 10:33.860
the p-value is just based on that tail,

10:33.860 --> 10:38.360
and we have a p-value of 0.01,

10:39.000 --> 10:42.340
so around 1%.

10:42.340 --> 10:44.160
So, that's quite strong evidence

10:44.160 --> 10:56.000
against the null hypothesis that the men and women's promotion times

10:56.000 --> 10:57.440
were from the same distribution.

10:59.720 --> 11:01.780
Notice that if we did a two-tailed test,

11:01.780 --> 11:06.660
we'd also arrive at a conclusion that there was evidence,

11:06.720 --> 11:07.900
although a little less strong,

11:08.600 --> 11:10.040
against the null hypothesis.

11:11.680 --> 11:13.440
Let's talk in generality now,

11:13.700 --> 11:17.280
and describe what we've done in the general case,

11:17.280 --> 11:20.540
where we've got two samples

11:20.540 --> 11:23.120
of size,

11:24.640 --> 11:29.100
let's call them M and N, respectively.

11:30.700 --> 11:33.320
So, I choose a test statistic.

11:33.320 --> 11:40.300
You can simply choose a test statistic

11:40.300 --> 11:45.560
that is a test statistic for a test that you already know,

11:45.720 --> 11:48.200
so you could choose a two-sample-t-test statistic,

11:48.960 --> 11:50.840
or rank sum, or whatever.

11:55.320 --> 11:57.780
We find, at least in principle,

11:57.780 --> 11:59.580
the test statistic

11:59.580 --> 12:03.120
that we chose

12:03.120 --> 12:05.160
for all

12:05.160 --> 12:06.240
of the

12:06.240 --> 12:08.100
n plus m

12:08.100 --> 12:09.060
to n

12:09.060 --> 12:13.220
regroupings

12:13.220 --> 12:15.920
of the data.

12:18.520 --> 12:20.060
So, all the ways in which

12:20.060 --> 12:22.320
we can assign the n plus m

12:22.320 --> 12:22.900
numbers

12:22.900 --> 12:25.800
into a group of n and a group of m.

12:27.780 --> 12:31.540
find the number

12:31.540 --> 12:33.480
of samples

12:33.480 --> 12:39.560
that have a test statistic

12:39.560 --> 12:47.360
at least

12:47.360 --> 12:49.540
as inconsistent

12:49.540 --> 12:54.740
with the null hypothesis

12:54.740 --> 13:00.300
as not observed.

13:01.720 --> 13:02.740
It's the one that we saw.

13:04.500 --> 13:06.600
And we divide that number

13:06.600 --> 13:12.300
so that count

13:12.300 --> 13:14.880
by

13:14.880 --> 13:17.020
n plus m

13:17.020 --> 13:17.800
to n

13:17.800 --> 13:22.100
to give our p-value.

13:23.500 --> 13:24.500
So, let's just

13:24.500 --> 13:25.880
talk a little bit more

13:25.880 --> 13:26.920
about our example

13:26.920 --> 13:28.980
in this

13:28.980 --> 13:30.160
Apache case.

13:30.820 --> 13:31.040
So,

13:31.720 --> 13:33.000
the data in the trial

13:33.000 --> 13:37.340
was such that

13:37.340 --> 13:38.260
if we choose

13:38.260 --> 13:38.780
t

13:38.780 --> 13:40.200
as our test statistic,

13:40.360 --> 13:41.180
let's say it's the

13:41.180 --> 13:49.820
total of the women's

13:49.820 --> 13:55.240
promotion times.

13:56.720 --> 13:57.800
We notice that

13:57.800 --> 13:58.480
if we look back

13:58.480 --> 13:59.060
at our data,

13:59.840 --> 14:00.680
the smallest value

14:00.680 --> 14:01.420
that could have taken

14:01.420 --> 14:02.620
across the regroupings

14:02.620 --> 14:04.640
is when the two

14:04.640 --> 14:05.560
smallest observations

14:05.560 --> 14:08.180
were for the women,

14:08.720 --> 14:10.180
which were five and seven.

14:10.440 --> 14:11.300
So, there's one

14:11.300 --> 14:13.780
out of the 276

14:13.780 --> 14:14.620
groupings

14:15.420 --> 14:16.320
that gives a value

14:16.320 --> 14:16.940
of 12

14:16.940 --> 14:17.900
for our test statistic.

14:18.700 --> 14:20.180
The next smallest value

14:20.180 --> 14:21.860
is 17.

14:22.660 --> 14:23.900
Again, there's only one way

14:23.900 --> 14:24.580
to do that.

14:24.580 --> 14:25.740
The next smallest value

14:25.740 --> 14:26.280
is 19

14:26.280 --> 14:27.040
and actually there's

14:27.040 --> 14:27.880
quite a few ways

14:27.880 --> 14:29.480
that can arise

14:29.480 --> 14:31.560
and so on.

14:32.040 --> 14:33.080
So, we get

14:33.080 --> 14:35.800
different numbers

14:35.800 --> 14:36.440
of ways.

14:39.300 --> 14:40.160
So, remember,

14:40.280 --> 14:41.020
this is the sampling

14:41.020 --> 14:41.980
distribution of this

14:41.980 --> 14:42.960
test statistic

14:42.960 --> 14:44.900
under the null hypothesis

14:44.900 --> 14:47.980
and we actually observed

14:47.980 --> 14:49.720
something way up

14:49.720 --> 14:50.440
in the upper tail,

14:50.900 --> 14:52.040
682,

14:52.720 --> 14:56.380
and we were doing

14:56.380 --> 14:57.600
a one tail test

14:57.600 --> 15:00.320
and those three

15:00.320 --> 15:01.200
cases together

15:01.200 --> 15:02.200
would only have

15:02.200 --> 15:02.780
probability

15:02.780 --> 15:05.700
of about 0.1

15:05.700 --> 15:08.060
if all of these

15:08.060 --> 15:08.740
possibilities,

15:09.320 --> 15:11.200
all of the 276

15:11.200 --> 15:12.100
were equally likely.

15:12.620 --> 15:14.280
So, that seems large,

15:14.340 --> 15:14.900
it seems to be

15:14.900 --> 15:16.040
way up in the upper tail

15:16.040 --> 15:17.260
if, in fact,

15:17.800 --> 15:20.140
T really was

15:20.140 --> 15:20.740
following this

15:20.740 --> 15:21.440
distribution

15:21.440 --> 15:25.220
under our null hypothesis.

15:25.220 --> 15:26.200
So, as always,

15:26.280 --> 15:26.960
when we're performing

15:26.960 --> 15:27.580
a test,

15:28.500 --> 15:29.340
we're considering

15:29.340 --> 15:30.420
how does that

15:30.420 --> 15:31.580
test statistic behave

15:31.580 --> 15:32.640
if the null hypothesis

15:32.640 --> 15:33.340
was true?

15:34.060 --> 15:34.660
And here,

15:34.760 --> 15:35.420
we're doing a brute

15:35.420 --> 15:36.200
force approach.

15:36.440 --> 15:37.240
We're actually

15:37.240 --> 15:39.300
computing this

15:39.300 --> 15:40.640
sampling distribution

15:40.640 --> 15:41.240
explicitly.

15:41.500 --> 15:42.040
We're really only

15:42.040 --> 15:43.000
interested in the tails,

15:43.140 --> 15:43.840
but we should have

15:43.840 --> 15:44.960
this picture in our mind.

15:47.760 --> 15:48.860
Interestingly enough,

15:49.760 --> 15:50.800
the Capacci case

15:50.800 --> 15:53.240
was defeated

15:53.240 --> 15:57.020
even, I understand,

15:57.360 --> 15:58.400
after an appeal.

16:00.220 --> 16:00.800
Of course,

16:01.040 --> 16:02.140
although these data

16:02.140 --> 16:02.960
look compelling,

16:03.600 --> 16:04.600
there's more to the data

16:04.600 --> 16:06.400
than meets the eye.

16:06.520 --> 16:07.240
There's always a story

16:07.240 --> 16:08.160
behind our data.

16:08.960 --> 16:10.380
And, of course,

16:11.000 --> 16:11.560
the data

16:11.560 --> 16:13.380
only included information

16:13.380 --> 16:14.020
for people

16:14.020 --> 16:15.080
who made it to promotion.

16:15.080 --> 16:16.380
So, there's no

16:16.380 --> 16:17.440
information about

16:17.440 --> 16:18.280
people who

16:18.280 --> 16:20.500
served the company

16:20.500 --> 16:21.440
and who didn't get

16:21.440 --> 16:22.420
promoted in that time.

16:22.880 --> 16:23.540
And there was also

16:23.540 --> 16:24.320
evidence that there

16:24.320 --> 16:25.340
were women who had

16:25.340 --> 16:27.400
turned down promotion,

16:27.620 --> 16:28.260
so had been offered

16:28.260 --> 16:29.740
promotion but refused it.

16:30.400 --> 16:31.440
I think on the grounds

16:31.440 --> 16:32.340
that the nature of the

16:32.340 --> 16:33.320
work changed once they

16:33.320 --> 16:33.920
got promoted

16:33.920 --> 16:35.600
and didn't suit

16:35.600 --> 16:36.000
some women.

16:36.840 --> 16:38.280
So, although the data

16:38.280 --> 16:39.040
looked quite,

16:39.380 --> 16:40.300
these data looked

16:40.300 --> 16:42.140
quite convincing,

16:42.140 --> 16:44.240
the case was

16:44.240 --> 16:46.500
defeated in the event.

16:48.400 --> 16:50.120
Now, let's just

16:50.120 --> 16:51.060
conclude here

16:51.060 --> 16:52.520
with a brief

16:52.520 --> 16:53.480
talk

16:53.480 --> 16:54.920
about

16:54.920 --> 16:56.500
the extent

16:56.500 --> 16:58.160
of statistical

16:58.160 --> 16:58.700
inference.

17:00.160 --> 17:00.880
Because, as we

17:00.880 --> 17:01.480
described

17:01.480 --> 17:02.680
in this example,

17:03.300 --> 17:04.220
the data were not

17:04.220 --> 17:04.820
a sample.

17:05.300 --> 17:05.720
So, normally,

17:05.820 --> 17:06.280
when we're doing

17:06.280 --> 17:06.760
statistics,

17:06.980 --> 17:07.500
we're thinking to

17:07.500 --> 17:08.360
ourselves that we

17:08.360 --> 17:09.400
have some sort

17:09.400 --> 17:10.760
of population,

17:10.760 --> 17:12.380
so some sort

17:12.380 --> 17:12.840
of set

17:12.840 --> 17:14.740
of individuals

17:14.740 --> 17:16.360
or objects

17:16.360 --> 17:17.380
or something,

17:18.540 --> 17:20.240
and we take

17:20.240 --> 17:22.000
a sample

17:22.000 --> 17:23.500
from that

17:23.500 --> 17:25.100
population.

17:26.180 --> 17:27.220
We hope,

17:29.200 --> 17:30.000
or it should be

17:30.000 --> 17:30.320
hoped,

17:30.700 --> 17:31.600
that we take

17:31.600 --> 17:32.100
the sample

17:32.100 --> 17:33.360
in some sensible

17:33.360 --> 17:34.780
way as to

17:34.780 --> 17:35.820
minimise bias.

17:36.280 --> 17:37.460
And a simple

17:37.460 --> 17:38.340
random sample,

17:39.080 --> 17:40.340
rather like a

17:40.340 --> 17:42.020
lottery or

17:42.020 --> 17:42.320
raffle,

17:42.700 --> 17:43.880
is the optimal

17:43.880 --> 17:44.660
type of sample

17:44.660 --> 17:45.240
to take.

17:45.640 --> 17:46.240
And if we were

17:46.240 --> 17:46.840
to take such a

17:46.840 --> 17:47.120
sample,

17:47.260 --> 17:48.200
then we'd be

17:48.200 --> 17:49.000
probably quite

17:49.000 --> 17:50.420
happy in

17:50.420 --> 17:51.960
drawing inference

17:51.960 --> 17:58.240
about our sample

17:58.240 --> 17:59.860
back to the

17:59.860 --> 18:00.420
population.

18:01.360 --> 18:02.240
More often,

18:02.580 --> 18:03.060
particularly when

18:03.060 --> 18:03.420
we're dealing

18:03.420 --> 18:04.100
with populations

18:04.100 --> 18:05.040
of human beings,

18:05.040 --> 18:06.260
it's very difficult

18:06.260 --> 18:07.860
to take a true

18:07.860 --> 18:08.900
random sample

18:08.900 --> 18:10.120
like a lottery.

18:10.640 --> 18:10.820
Now,

18:10.860 --> 18:11.020
in the

18:11.020 --> 18:11.720
Capaccio case,

18:12.600 --> 18:13.260
the data were

18:13.260 --> 18:14.060
not a sample.

18:14.980 --> 18:15.420
So,

18:15.640 --> 18:16.140
the inference,

18:16.240 --> 18:16.620
if anything,

18:16.920 --> 18:17.800
was about the

18:17.800 --> 18:19.240
process which gave

18:19.240 --> 18:19.880
rise to the

18:19.880 --> 18:20.720
promotion times.

18:21.140 --> 18:21.340
So,

18:21.400 --> 18:21.900
whatever the

18:21.900 --> 18:22.980
processes were,

18:23.260 --> 18:23.840
or if there was

18:23.840 --> 18:24.760
a single process,

18:25.260 --> 18:26.700
really did it differ

18:26.700 --> 18:27.620
for the men

18:27.620 --> 18:28.060
and the women.

18:28.520 --> 18:28.860
So,

18:28.860 --> 18:29.620
we can imagine

18:29.620 --> 18:30.420
some mechanism,

18:30.740 --> 18:32.240
some pseudo-random

18:32.240 --> 18:33.120
process which is

18:33.120 --> 18:34.080
giving rise to the

18:34.080 --> 18:34.420
data,

18:34.420 --> 18:36.040
and we were

18:36.040 --> 18:36.720
interested in,

18:36.860 --> 18:37.000
well,

18:37.360 --> 18:38.560
however this process

18:38.560 --> 18:39.160
operated,

18:39.640 --> 18:40.420
is it giving the

18:40.420 --> 18:41.300
same sort of data

18:41.300 --> 18:42.160
for men as it did

18:42.160 --> 18:42.580
for women?

18:45.180 --> 18:45.680
So,

18:45.740 --> 18:46.060
there was no

18:46.060 --> 18:46.980
sample there

18:46.980 --> 18:47.400
as such,

18:47.460 --> 18:48.260
unless one thinks

18:48.260 --> 18:50.840
of hypothetical

18:50.840 --> 18:52.600
individuals who

18:52.600 --> 18:53.480
might have worked

18:53.480 --> 18:53.960
for the company

18:53.960 --> 18:54.540
but didn't,

18:54.900 --> 18:55.560
and their promotion

18:55.560 --> 18:57.060
times we weren't

18:57.060 --> 18:57.620
observed,

18:57.740 --> 18:58.560
but we actually

18:58.560 --> 18:59.300
observed some

18:59.300 --> 19:01.020
subset of people

19:01.020 --> 19:02.320
who did work

19:02.320 --> 19:03.240
for the company.

19:04.420 --> 19:04.980
Now,

19:05.700 --> 19:06.240
extending the

19:06.240 --> 19:06.780
idea a little

19:06.780 --> 19:07.400
further,

19:07.900 --> 19:09.900
we'll discuss

19:09.900 --> 19:11.180
in the course

19:11.180 --> 19:12.080
examples of

19:12.080 --> 19:13.520
what we would

19:13.520 --> 19:14.380
describe as

19:14.380 --> 19:15.320
experiments.

19:15.940 --> 19:16.100
So,

19:16.180 --> 19:17.020
sometimes we

19:17.020 --> 19:19.160
don't just

19:19.160 --> 19:19.620
stop at

19:19.620 --> 19:20.120
taking a

19:20.120 --> 19:21.660
sample and

19:21.660 --> 19:23.420
observing what

19:23.420 --> 19:24.280
we've seen

19:24.280 --> 19:25.000
from the sample,

19:25.400 --> 19:26.360
we do some

19:26.360 --> 19:26.680
sort of

19:26.680 --> 19:27.180
experiment,

19:27.380 --> 19:27.680
and an

19:27.680 --> 19:28.100
experiment

19:28.100 --> 19:28.780
involves,

19:30.260 --> 19:30.740
in the

19:30.740 --> 19:31.440
simplest form,

